("premiere",None,"Le nozze di Figaro premiered at the Burgtheater in Vienna on 1 May 1786.","It premiered at the Burgtheater in Vienna on 1 May 1786."),
("history",None,"Beaumarchais\u2019s play was at first banned in Vienna for its frank treatment of class conflict; Da Ponte won the emperor\u2019s approval for an opera version.",["with its frank treatment of class conflict, was at first banned in Vienna","Lorenzo Da Ponte managed to get official approval from the emperor for an operatic version"],"Composition_history"),
("history",None,"It was Mozart who picked Beaumarchais\u2019s play. Da Ponte turned it into a libretto in six weeks, removing all of the political references.","It was Mozart who originally selected Beaumarchais's play and brought it to Da Ponte, who turned it into a libretto in six weeks, rewriting it in poetic Italian and removing all of the original's political references","Composition_history"),
("history","Aprite un po|Tutto \u00e8 disposto|Tutto e disposto","Da Ponte swapped Figaro\u2019s angry speech against inherited nobility for an equally angry aria against unfaithful wives.","Da Ponte replaced Figaro's climactic speech against inherited nobility with an equally angry aria against unfaithful wives","Composition_history"),
("music","Aprite un po","Mozart uses two horns playing together here to represent cuckoldry. Verdi later borrowed the same trick in Falstaff.",["Mozart uses the sound of two horns playing together to represent cuckoldry in the act 4 aria \"Aprite un po' quegli occhi\"","Verdi later used the same device in Ford's aria in Falstaff."],"Critical_discussion"),
("premiere",None,"The first-night audience demanded encores of five numbers; on 8 May, seven.","The applause of the audience on the first night resulted in five numbers being encored, seven on 8 May.","Performance_history"),
("history",None,"Worried about the running time, Emperor Joseph II ordered posters announcing that no piece for more than a single voice was to be repeated.",["Emperor Joseph, in charge of the Burgtheater, was concerned by the length of the performance","no piece for more than a single voice is to be repeated"],"Performance_history"),
("critic",None,"A 1786 Vienna review: Mozart\u2019s music was admired by connoisseurs, but \u201cobstreperous louts in the uppermost storey\u201d tried to drown out the singers.",["Mozart's music was generally admired by connoisseurs already at the first performance","obstreperous louts in the uppermost storey exerted their hired lungs with all their might to deafen singers and audience alike"],"Performance_history","Wiener Realzeitung, 11 July 1786 (via Wikipedia)"),
("premiere",None,"In Prague the opera caused a sensation; a local paper wrote that \u201cno piece \u2026 has ever caused such a sensation.\u201d That success led to the commission of Don Giovanni.",["no piece (for everyone here asserts) has ever caused such a sensation","The success of the Prague production led to the commissioning of the next Mozart/Da Ponte opera, Don Giovanni"],"Other_early_performances","Prager Oberpostamtszeitung (via Wikipedia)"),
("music","Finale|Esci omai|Esci ormai|Susanna, or via","The Act II finale runs about 20 minutes, one of the longest uninterrupted pieces of music Mozart ever wrote.","The finale of act 2, lasting 20 minutes, is one of the longest uninterrupted pieces of music Mozart ever wrote.","Critical_discussion"),
("critic",None,"Brahms: \u201cIn my opinion, each number in Figaro is a miracle \u2026 nothing like it was ever done again, not even by Beethoven.\u201d",["In my opinion, each number in Figaro is a miracle","nothing like it was ever done again, not even by Beethoven"],"Critical_discussion","Johannes Brahms (via Wikipedia)"),
("music","Non pi\u00f9 andrai|Non piu andrai","Mozart liked this tune enough to quote \u201cNon pi\u00f9 andrai\u201d in the second act of Don Giovanni.","Mozart also quotes Figaro's aria \"Non più andrai\" in the second act of his opera Don Giovanni.","Other_uses_of_the_melodies"),
("music","Porgi,? amor","\u201cPorgi, amor\u201d reuses the motif that opens Mozart\u2019s early bassoon concerto.","Mozart also reused the motif that begins his early bassoon concerto in another aria sung by the countess, \"Porgi, amor\".","Other_uses_of_the_melodies"),
("music","Cosa sento|Cos\u00ec fan tutte|Cosi fan tutte","Basilio\u2019s phrase \u201cCos\u00ec fan tutte le belle\u201d in this trio later turned up in the overture to Mozart\u2019s Cos\u00ec fan tutte.","A musical phrase from the act 1 trio of The Marriage of Figaro (where Basilio sings Così fan tutte le belle) was later reused by Mozart in the overture to his opera Così fan tutte.","Other_uses_of_the_melodies"),
